### stage-refs-curator/SKILL.md
--- 1
**Fanning the read out (Principle 9).** More than one paper in a run — an `add` list, or the batch a `discover` run's user picked — splits one delegate per paper — `subagent` subagents (`subagent_type: general-purpose`, `model:` the EXEC tier value, omitted when empty), dispatched as their records resolve, never more at once than the width fixed under §6.2 before the first goes out. No question precedes it (§6.1); a host that offers no dispatch, or refuses the call, falls back to reading them here, and the digest says so. Items 1 and 2 still run at home — the bibliographic record is what the entry is transcribed from, and no delegate fetches one — but not for the whole list before the first reader starts. Per batch — one paper, or as many as the §6.2 width allows, as the main agent judges — resolve the records, transcribe the entries, assign the notes' filenames, dispatch that batch's readers in one message (§6.1), then begin the next batch's records. The two streams reach different hosts — records to DBLP and Crossref, pages to arXiv and the venue sites — so neither spends the other's rate and a backoff on one no longer stops the other, where resolving every record first left every reader idle through a rate-limit storm at DBLP. Item 3 is what travels, and each brief carries its paper's page candidates in the order item 3 names them, the seconds it waits between its own requests to a host (`references/source-policy.md`, Rate limits), its own cache prefix under `wkdrs/refs_<date>/raw/`, the note path it is to write, the collector contract verbatim, the story and the claim ledger so `## Relation to ours` is written where the paper was read, and the scope line "ONLY this paper; fetch only its pages; write only that one note file". At involve `high`, announce the partition before dispatch (§6.8). Item 4 then runs at home over what came back, per paper: the index row and the `% src:` line, the impact score, and the batch self-check — all in files no delegate opens. A return whose `floor_evidence` says `not reached` wrote no note and leaves the bib entry standing alone, which is item 3's unfetchable path in fanned-out form; a return claiming the floor was reached while naming no note file contradicts itself, and that paper is read again here (§6.3).
+++ 1
